  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DEFENDING SUBSISTENCE FARMERS AND PROTECTING FOREST ELEPHANTS USING MOBILE ELECTRIC FENCES - BUILDING ON USFWS FUNDED PROJECT F22AP00424</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE PURPOSE OF THIS PROJECT IS TO SUPPORT AN ELEPHANT CONSERVATION EFFORT IN GABON BY REDUCING HUMAN-ELEPHANT CONFLICT (HEC) AND IMPROVING LOCAL LIVELIHOODS. GABON IS THE PRIMARY REMAINING STRONGHOLD FOR THE SPECIES, WITH AN ESTIMATED 95,110 ELEPHANTS (OVER HALF THE REMAINING FOREST ELEPHANT POPULATION). THIS PROJECT IS INTENDED TO CONSERVE AFRICAN FOREST ELEPHANTS AND THEIR HABITAT BY ADDRESSING THE IMPACTS OF HEC. ACTIVITIES INCLUDE: (1) DEPLOYING AND OR ADAPTING 100 MOBILE ELEPHANT-PROOF ELECTRIC FENCES ANNUALLY IN THE NYANGA PROVINCE (2) INSTALLING A NEW PROVINCIAL MOBILE UNIT OFFICE AND RECRUITING AND TRAINING OFFICE STAFF (3) PROVIDING TRAINING AND CAPACITY BUILDING FOR COMMUNITY BENEFICIARIES AND GOVERNMENT STAKEHOLDERS, INCLUDING FORMAL AND INFORMAL TRAINING WORKSHOPS AND DEVELOPMENT OF A FENCE-USER MANUAL AND ANNOTATED BEST PRACTICES GUIDE AND (4) DEVELOPING AND DISTRIBUTING FENCING OUTREACH MATERIALS IN NYANGA PROVINCE.</narrative>
  </description>
